
  [1mB. Pure core + runtime adapters.[0m [38;5;153msrc/logger/core.js[39m holds only pure functions — build a record, decide if a level
  passes, scrub for console, project the allowlist for remote — with no I/O and no runtime detection. [38;5;153msrc/logger/node.js
[39m  and [38;5;153msrc/logger/browser.js[39m are thin: pick a sink, install global handlers, read level config. Each file carries the
  same small dual-mode tail, so the browser loads core then adapter as two ordered script tags and Node just requires
  the adapter. Trade-off: three files instead of one, and the browser depends on script order. [3mComplexity: moderate.[0m

  [1mC. ES modules everywhere.[0m Same layering as B, but convert the project to ESM — [38;5;153m"type": "module"[39m in [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m,
  rewrite [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m and [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m off [38;5;153mrequire[39m, and change [38;5;153mindex.html[39m to [38;5;153m<script type="module">[39m. Real imports, no
  globals, no interop tail, and it's the natural base if you later add a bundler or a test runner. Trade-off: it
  rewrites existing files that have nothing to do with logging, and [38;5;153mtype="module"[39m is fetched under CORS rules, so
  opening [38;5;153mindex.html[39m via [38;5;153mfile://[39m stops working — you'd need to serve the page. That's a change to how the app is run,
  not just how it's built. [3mComplexity: moderate, plus a workflow change.[0m

  My recommendation is [1mB[0m. The allowlist redaction boundary is the one piece of this design where a mistake is a
  disclosure rather than a bug, and isolating it as a pure function makes it exhaustively testable with no DOM, no
  network, and no process — which is exactly what you want guarding that boundary. B also leaves [38;5;153mfile://[39m working and
  doesn't touch [38;5;153mutils.js[39m or the existing entry point. A is tempting for a repo this small, but it puts the
  security-critical logic in the same file as the transport, which is where I'd least like it. C's ESM cleanliness is
  real, but it buys module hygiene at the cost of rewriting unrelated files and changing how you open the page — worth
  doing when you adopt a bundler, not as a side effect of adding logging.
